The final match of the [[2014 FIFA World Cup]], the 20th edition of [[FIFA]]'s competition for national [[association football|football]] teams, was played at the [[Maracanã Stadium]] in [[Rio de Janeiro]], Brazil, on 13 July 2014, and was contested by [[Germany national football team|Germany]] and [[Argentina national football team|Argentina]]. 

The event comprised hosts [[Brazil national football team|Brazil]] and 31 other teams who emerged from [[2014 FIFA World Cup qualification|the qualification phase]], organised by the six [[List of football federations#FIFA Confederations|FIFA confederations]]. The 32 teams competed in a group stage, from which 16 teams qualified for [[2014 FIFA World Cup knockout stage|the knockout stage]]. En route to the final, Germany finished first in [[2014 FIFA World Cup Group G|Group G]], with two wins and a draw, after which they defeated [[Algeria national football team|Algeria]] in the round of 16, [[France national football team|France]] in the quarter-final and Brazil, by [[Brazil v Germany (2014 FIFA World Cup)|a score of {{nowrap|7–1}}]], in the semi-final. Argentina finished first in [[2014 FIFA World Cup Group F|Group F]] with three wins, before defeating [[Switzerland national football team|Switzerland]] in the round of 16, [[Belgium national football team|Belgium]] in the quarter-final and the [[Netherlands national football team|Netherlands]] in a [[penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]] in the semi-final. The final was witnessed by 74,738 spectators in the stadium, as well as over a billion watching on television, with the referee for the match being Nicola Rizzoli from Italy.

[[Gonzalo Higuaín]] missed a chance to score for Argentina in the first half when he was [[Glossary of association football terms#O|one-on-one]] with Germany goalkeeper [[Manuel Neuer]], and [[Benedikt Höwedes]] failed to give Germany the lead shortly before half-time when his shot struck the goalpost. [[Lionel Messi]] had an opportunity to score when he was one-on-one with Neuer shortly after half time, but his low shot went wide of the goal. On 71 minutes, [[Thomas Müller]] was through on goal following a build-up involving [[André Schürrle]] and [[Mesut Özil]], but he failed to control the ball and lost it to Argentina's goalkeeper, [[Sergio Romero]]. With the match goalless after 90 minutes, it went to [[Overtime (sports)|extra time]], in the second period of which Germany broke the deadlock. [[Mario Götze]], who had come on as a [[Substitute (association football)|substitute]] shortly before the end of normal time, received Schürrle's [[Cross (association football)|cross]] from the left on his chest before [[Volley (association football)|volleying]] a left-footed shot into the net to secure a 1–0 victory for Germany.

Germany's win was their [[List of FIFA World Cup finals|fourth World Cup title]] and the first since [[German reunification]], as well as the first World Cup win by a European team in the [[Americas]]. Götze was named the [[Player of the match|man of the match]], and Messi was awarded the [[FIFA World Cup awards#Golden Ball|Golden Ball]] as FIFA's outstanding player of the tournament. Germany's [[Manager (association football)|manager]], [[Joachim Löw]], labelled his side's win as the culmination of a project that had begun ten years previously under his predecessor [[Jürgen Klinsmann]], and praised his team's spirit. His Argentine counterpart, [[Alejandro Sabella]], thought his team had been unlucky to lose, and called his players "warriors". Germany failed to defend their trophy at the subsequent [[2018 FIFA World Cup|2018 World Cup]] in Russia, becoming the third successive World Cup holders [[2018 FIFA World Cup Group F|to be eliminated in the group phase]] after defeats against [[Mexico national football team|Mexico]] and [[South Korea national football team|South Korea]].

==Background==
[[File:Deutsches Fußballmuseum 2015 3.jpg|thumb|The Adidas Brazuca Final Rio used in the match|alt=View of the match ball, displayed on a pedestal]]
The [[2014 FIFA World Cup]] was the 20th edition of the [[FIFA World Cup|World Cup]], [[FIFA]]'s football competition for national teams, held in Brazil between 12 June and 13 July 2014.<ref name="Britanica">{{Cite encyclopedia |url-status=live |title=World Cup: History & Winners |last1=Tikkanen |first1=Amy |last2=Augustyn |first2=Adam |last3=Levy |first3=Michael |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210828023709/https://www.britannica.com/sports/World-Cup-football |archive-date=28 August 2021 |last4=Ray |first4=Michael |last5=Luebering |first5=J. E. |last6=Lotha |first6=Gloria |last7=Young |first7=Grace |last8=Shepherd |first8=Melinda C. |last9=Sinha |first9=Surabhi |last10=Rodriguez |first10=Emily |encyclopedia=[[Encyclopædia Britannica]] |date=19 August 2021 |access-date=22 September 2021 |url=https://www.britannica.com/sports/World-Cup-football }}</ref><ref name="RSSSF_summary">{{Cite web |title=World Cup 2014 |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |date=5 July 2018 |access-date=22 September 2021 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/2014f.html |archive-date=19 October 2018 |publisher=[[RSSSF]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181019014641/http://www.rsssf.com/tables/2014f.html |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Brazil national football team|Brazil]] qualified for the finals automatically as tournament hosts, and 203 teams competed for the remaining 31 spots through [[2014 FIFA World Cup qualification|qualifying rounds]] organised by the six [[List of football federations#FIFA Confederations|FIFA confederations]] and held between June 2011 and November 2013.<ref name="France24Qualif">{{Cite web |title=FIFA to unveil 2014 World Cup qualification draw |work=[[France 24]] |date=29 July 2011 |access-date=22 September 2021 |url=https://www.france24.com/en/20110729-fifa-unveil-2014-world-cup-qualification-draw |archive-date=26 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210526042059/https://www.france24.com/en/20110729-fifa-unveil-2014-world-cup-qualification-draw |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup 2014 Qualifying |last1=Stokkermans |first1=Karel |last2=Andrés |first2=Juan Pablo |last3=Lugo |first3=Erik Francisco |date=16 November 2017 |access-date=22 September 2021 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/2014q.html |archive-date=26 June 2016 |publisher=[[RSSSF]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160626094221/http://www.rsssf.com/tables/2014q.html |url-status=live }}</ref> In the finals, the teams were divided into eight groups of four with each team playing each other once in a [[round-robin tournament|round-robin]] format. The two top teams from each group advanced to a [[Single-elimination tournament|knock-out phase]].<ref name="RSSSF_summary"/> The defending champions from the [[2010 FIFA World Cup|2010 World Cup]] were [[Spain national football team|Spain]], who had achieved three successive victories in major tournaments, having also won the [[UEFA European Championship]] in [[UEFA Euro 2008|2008]] and [[UEFA Euro 2012|2012]].<ref name="BBCThreeInaRow">{{Cite web |title=Are Spain the best team of all time? |work=[[BBC Sport]] |date=2 July 2012 |access-date=20 July 2021 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/18669029 |archive-date=3 December 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201203142901/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/18669029 |url-status=live }}</ref> They were eliminated in the [[2014 FIFA World Cup Group B|group stage]] this year.<ref name="RSSSF_summary"/>

[[Germany national football team|Germany]] had won the title three times before as West Germany – in [[1954 FIFA World Cup|1954]], [[1974 FIFA World Cup|1974]] and [[1990 FIFA World Cup|1990]]. [[Argentina national football team|Argentina]] had two titles, in [[1978 FIFA World Cup|1978]] and [[1986 FIFA World Cup|1986]].<ref name="Britanica"/> The two sides had met each other six times previously in the World Cup,<ref>{{Cite web |title=Germany national football team: record v Argentina |work=11v11.com |publisher=AFS Enterprises |access-date=22 September 2021 |url=https://www.11v11.com/teams/germany/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Argentina/ |archive-date=29 April 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210429040724/https://www.11v11.com/teams/germany/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Argentina/ |url-status=live }}</ref> including in the [[1986 FIFA World Cup final|1986 final]], which Argentina won {{nowrap|3–2}}, and the [[1990 FIFA World Cup final|1990 final]], which West Germany won {{nowrap|1–0}}.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/worldcup/mexico1986/matches/round=308/match=393/index.html|title=1986 FIFA World Cup Match Report: Argentina – Germany FR |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association|access-date=2 November 2017|archive-date=9 November 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141109113051/http://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/worldcup/mexico1986/matches/round=308/match=393/index.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/worldcup/italy1990/matches/round=3462/match=27/index.html|title=1990 FIFA World Cup Match Report: Germany FR – Argentina |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association|access-date=2 November 2017|archive-date=27 December 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141227000251/http://www.fifa.com/tournaments/archive/worldcup/italy1990/matches/round=3462/match=27/index.html|url-status=live}}</ref> They had met in the quarter-final of both of the most recent two World Cups, Germany winning both – [[2006 FIFA World Cup knockout stage#Germany vs Argentina|a 4–2 penalty shoot-out win in 2006]] and [[2010 FIFA World Cup knockout stage#Argentina vs Germany|a {{nowrap|4–0}} win in 2010]]. Germany then lost in the semi-finals and finished third on both occasions.<ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup 2006 |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |date=5 July 2018 |access-date=22 September 2021 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/2006f.html |archive-date=17 August 2007 |publisher=[[RSSSF]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070817005955/http://www.rsssf.com/tables/2006f.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup 2010 |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |date=5 July 2018 |access-date=22 September 2021 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/2010f.html |archive-date=7 June 2021 |publisher=[[RSSSF]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210607074456/http://www.rsssf.com/tables/2010f.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The most recent meeting between the two teams before the 2014 World Cup was a [[Exhibition game|friendly]] match played on 15 August 2012 at [[Commerzbank-Arena]] in Frankfurt, which was won by Argentina {{nowrap|3–1}}.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.goal.com/en-ie/match/germany-vs-argentina/1213336/report |title=Germany 1 Argentina 3 |date=15 August 2012 |website=[[Goal (website)|Goal]] |access-date=9 July 2014 |archive-date=26 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140726073753/http://www.goal.com/en-ie/match/germany-vs-argentina/1213336/report |url-status=live }}</ref>

The venue for the final was announced in 2011 as the Estádio Jornalista Mário Filho in [[Rio de Janeiro]], commonly known as the [[Maracanã Stadium]].<ref>{{Cite book |title=Pelé : Soccer Star & Ambassador |last=Trusdell |first=Brian |publisher=ABDO Publishing Company |year=2014 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=hMBxAgAAQBAJ&pg=PA29 |page=29 |isbn=978-1-629-68148-1 |access-date=23 September 2021 |archive-date=23 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210923190322/https://books.google.co.uk/books?id=hMBxAgAAQBAJ&pg=PA29 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Maracana to host final |work=[[Sky Sports]] |date=29 July 2011 |access-date=23 September 2021 |url=https://www.skysports.com/football/news/12098/7066647/maracana-to-host-final |archive-date=10 March 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120310073449/http://www1.skysports.com/football/news/12098/7066647/Maracana-to-host-final |url-status=live }}</ref> It was built in preparation for the [[1950 FIFA World Cup]], and [[Uruguay v Brazil (1950 FIFA World Cup)|that tournament's final match]], in which [[Uruguay national football team|Uruguay]] beat Brazil {{nowrap|2–1}} to win the trophy, is {{as of|2021|lc=on}} the record-holder for the highest attendance at any football match in history.{{efn|''[[Guinness World Records]]'' state that the official crowd for the game was 173,850, but note that with illegal entrants included, who were not recorded, the figure could have been as high as 210,000.<ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup Rewind: Largest attendance at a match in the 1950 Brazil final |last=Janela |first=Mike |publisher=[[Guinness World Records]] |date=12 June 2018 |access-date=23 September 2021 |url=https://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/news/2018/6/world-cup-rewind-world-cup-rewind-largest-attendance-at-a-match-in-the-1950-brazil-final |archive-date=27 April 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190427181702/http://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/news/2018/6/world-cup-rewind-world-cup-rewind-largest-attendance-at-a-match-in-the-1950-brazil-final |url-status=live }}</ref>}}<ref>{{Cite web |title=Fans back in stadiums: The post-war football fandom boom in the late 1940s suggests the game will recover from the COVID-19 pandemic |last=Brown |first=Paul |work=[[FourFourTwo]] |date=3 September 2021 |access-date=23 September 2021 |url=https://www.fourfourtwo.com/features/fans-back-in-stadiums-the-post-war-football-fandom-boom-in-the-late-1940s-suggests-the-game-will-recover-from-the-covid-19-pandemic |archive-date=7 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210907194621/https://www.fourfourtwo.com/features/fans-back-in-stadiums-the-post-war-football-fandom-boom-in-the-late-1940s-suggests-the-game-will-recover-from-the-covid-19-pandemic |url-status=live }}</ref> The Maracanã also hosted the finals of the [[Copa América]] in [[1989 Copa América|1989]], [[2019 Copa América final|2019]] and [[2021 Copa América final|2021]]; the [[2013 FIFA Confederations Cup final]]; and both the men's and women's football finals [[Football at the 2016 Summer Olympics|at the 2016 Summer Olympics]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Brazil v Argentina is a final that could redeem a disappointing Copa América |last=Sanderson |first=Tom |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=9 July 2021 |access-date=23 September 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2021/jul/09/brazil-argentina-final-redeem-copa-america-maracana |archive-date=21 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210921002512/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2021/jul/09/brazil-argentina-final-redeem-copa-america-maracana |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.espn.co.uk/football/mens-olympic-tournament/story/2709112/maracana-to-host-mens-and-womens-olympic-football-finals |publisher=[[ESPN]] |date=11 November 2015 |title=Maracana to host 2016 Men's and Women's Olympic Football finals |access-date=23 September 2021 |archive-date=23 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210923190255/https://www.espn.co.uk/football/mens-olympic-tournament/story/2709112/maracana-to-host-mens-and-womens-olympic-football-finals |url-status=live }}</ref> The stadium underwent an extensive rebuild before the 2014 World Cup, after which its capacity was 78,838.<ref>{{Cite web |title=In pictures: Maracana stadium reopens |work=BBC News |date=28 April 2013 |access-date=23 September 2021 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/in-pictures-22312269 |archive-date=24 February 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210224174200/https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/in-pictures-22312269 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="maximumcapacityestadão">{{cite web|title=Maracanã fica mais moderno sem abrir mão de sua história |url=http://www.estadao.com.br/especiais/maracana-fica-mais-moderno-sem-abrir-mao-de-sua-historia,182163.htm |access-date=22 September 2012 |language=pt |work=[[O Estado de S. Paulo]] |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120922062246/http://www.estadao.com.br/especiais/maracana-fica-mais-moderno-sem-abrir-mao-de-sua-historia%2C182163.htm |archive-date=22 September 2012 }}</ref>

The match ball for the 2014 FIFA World Cup final, announced on 29 May 2014, was a variation of the [[Adidas Brazuca]] named the ''Adidas Brazuca Final Rio''.<ref name=brazucafinalrio>{{cite web |title=Adidas Brazuca Final Rio unveiled |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2014/m=5/news=adidas-brazuca-final-rio-unveiled-2345973.html |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |access-date=14 July 2014 |date=29 May 2014 |archive-date=1 June 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140601031750/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2014/m=5/news=adidas-brazuca-final-rio-unveiled-2345973.html |url-status=dead}}</ref> Though the technical aspects of the ball were the same, the design was different from the Brazuca balls used in the group phase and prior knockout games, with a green, gold and black coloring.<ref name=brazucafinalrio/> It was the third ball released specifically for FIFA World Cup final matches, after the [[Adidas Teamgeist|Teamgeist Berlin]] (2006) and the [[Adidas Jabulani|Jo'bulani]] (2010).<ref>{{Cite news |title=World Cup Ball – What Ball Will Be Used At The 2018 World Cup? |work=[[World Soccer (magazine)|World Soccer]] |date=13 July 2018 |access-date=26 September 2021 |url=https://www.worldsoccer.com/world-cup/world-cup-ball-401201 |archive-date=21 January 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210121185532/https://www.worldsoccer.com/world-cup/world-cup-ball-401201 |url-status=live }}</ref>

