Germany were drawn in [[2014 FIFA World Cup Group G|Group G]] for the World Cup, in which they were joined by [[Ghana national football team|Ghana]], [[Portugal national football team|Portugal]] and the [[United States men's national soccer team|United States]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Guide to World Cup Group G |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=7 June 2014 |access-date=12 September 2021 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25201600 |archive-date=12 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210912115229/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25201600 |url-status=live }}</ref> Their first match was against Portugal, on 16 June 2014 at the [[Itaipava Arena Fonte Nova]] in [[Salvador, Bahia|Salvador]]. Germany won a [[Penalty kick (association football)|penalty]] on 10 minutes, when [[João Pereira (footballer, born 1984)|João Pereira]] fouled [[Mario Götze]]. Müller took the kick and gave Germany the lead.<ref name="EurosportGermanyPortugal">{{Cite web |title=Germany – Portugal live |publisher=[[Eurosport]] |date=16 June 2014 |access-date=12 September 2021 |url=https://www.eurosport.com/football/world-cup/2014/live-germany-portugal_mtc491979/live.shtml |archive-date=18 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210918095942/https://www.eurosport.com/geoblocking.shtml |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Mats Hummels]] added a second goal with a [[Header (association football)|header]] on 32 minutes, and Portugal's [[Pepe (footballer, born 1983)|Pepe]] was shown a [[Penalty card#Red card|red card]] five minutes later, after he [[headbutt]]ed Müller.<ref name="Ornstein4Nil">{{Cite web |title=Germany 4–0 Portugal |last=Ornstein |first=David |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=16 June 2014 |access-date=12 September 2021 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25285106 |archive-date=5 June 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210605202452/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25285106 |url-status=live }}</ref> Müller then scored his second in [[injury time]] to give Germany a 3–0 half-time lead.<ref name="Ornstein4Nil"/> Müller scored a third from close range in the second half, to complete a 4–0 victory, after [[Rui Patrício]] had failed to clear a [[Cross (association football)|cross]] by [[André Schürrle]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Germany 4–0 Portugal |last=Wilson |first=Paul |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=16 June 2014 |access-date=12 September 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jun/16/germany-portugal-world-cup-2014-group-g-match-report |archive-date=12 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210912131209/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jun/16/germany-portugal-world-cup-2014-group-g-match-report |url-status=live }}</ref> Germany's second game was against Ghana at the [[Estádio Castelão]] in [[Fortaleza]]. After a goalless first half, Götze opened the scoring on 51 minutes, when his headed [[Shooting (association football)|shot]] from a Müller cross bounced off his own knee and past Ghana's goalkeeper [[Fatau Dauda]]. Ghana equalised shortly afterwards, through an [[André Ayew]] header, and then took the lead when [[Sulley Muntari]] found [[Asamoah Gyan]] with a [[Passing (association football)|pass]], after an error by [[Manuel Neuer]].<ref name="BBCGermanyGhana">{{Cite web |title=World Cup: Germany 2–2 Ghana |last=Smith |first=Ben |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=21 June 2014 |access-date=12 September 2021 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25285228 |archive-date=12 November 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201112023204/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25285228 |url-status=live }}</ref> Germany levelled the game again on 71 minutes, when [[Miroslav Klose]], who had come on as a [[Substitute (association football)|substitute]], kicked [[Benedikt Höwedes]]'s goal-bound header into the goal from close range.<ref name="BBCGermanyGhana"/><ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup: Germany 2–2 Ghana; Klose equals record |work=[[The Scotsman]] |date=21 June 2014 |access-date=12 September 2021 |url=https://www.scotsman.com/sport/football/world-cup-germany-2-2-ghana-klose-equals-record-1533477 |archive-date=12 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210912151412/https://www.scotsman.com/sport/football/world-cup-germany-2-2-ghana-klose-equals-record-1533477 |url-status=live }}</ref> Klose's goal equalled the Brazilian [[Ronaldo (Brazilian footballer)|Ronaldo]]'s World Cup record of 15 goals, and the game finished 2–2.<ref name="BBCGermanyGhana"/> Germany's last group game was against the United States at [[Itaipava Arena Pernambuco]] in [[Recife]], with a win or draw needed to guarantee qualification.<ref>{{Cite web |title=USA v Germany: World Cup 2014 – as it happened |last=Parker |first=Graham |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=26 June 2014 |access-date=12 September 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jun/26/usa-v-germany-world-cup-2014-live |archive-date=12 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210912153526/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jun/26/usa-v-germany-world-cup-2014-live |url-status=live }}</ref> In rainy conditions, Müller scored the only goal of the game with a shot inside the [[penalty area]] after goalkeeper [[Tim Howard]] had saved [[Per Mertesacker]]'s header. Germany qualified as group winners.<ref>{{Cite web |title=USA 0–1 Germany |last=Smith |first=Ben |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=26 June 2014 |access-date=12 September 2021 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25285382 |archive-date=18 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140718183358/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/25285382 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Germany's opponents in [[2014 FIFA World Cup knockout stage|the round of 16]] were [[Algeria national football team|Algeria]], with the match played on 30 June at the [[Estádio Beira-Rio]] in [[Porto Alegre]].<ref name="GuardianGermanyAlgeria">{{Cite web |title=Germany 2–1 Algeria |last=Hytner |first=David |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=30 June 2014 |access-date=13 September 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jun/30/germany-algeria-world-cup-last-16-match-report |archive-date=26 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210526183844/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jun/30/germany-algeria-world-cup-last-16-match-report |url-status=live }}</ref> The game was tied at 0–0 after 90 minutes, Germany being described by [[BBC Sport]]'s David Ornstein as looking frequently "rattled" as "Algeria wasted a succession of chances in an opening, exciting encounter".<ref name="GermanyAlgeriaBBC">{{Cite web |title=Germany 2–1 Algeria |first=David |last=Ornstein |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=30 June 2014 |access-date=13 September 2021 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/27969924 |archive-date=12 November 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201112033047/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/27969924 |url-status=live }}</ref> Germany took the lead early in [[Overtime (sports)|extra time]], when Müller crossed from the left and Schürrle scored from close range.<ref name="GermanyAlgeriaBBC"/> [[Mesut Özil]] added a second for Germany in the final minute of extra time, before [[Abdelmoumene Djabou]] pulled a goal back for Algeria with a [[Volley (association football)|volley]] in injury time. Germany held on to record a 2–1 win.
<ref name="GuardianGermanyAlgeria"/> They then faced [[France national football team|France]] in the quarter-final at the Maracanã Stadium on 4 July. Hummels gave Germany the lead on 12 minutes with a header, following a free kick by [[Toni Kroos]], and that proved to be the only goal of what Ornstein described as a "comfortable" win.<ref>{{Cite web |title=France 0–1 Germany |last=Ornstein |first=David |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=4 July 2014 |access-date=13 September 2021 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/28051800 |archive-date=23 November 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151123142443/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/28051800 |url-status=live }}</ref> Germany faced tournament hosts Brazil in their semi-final game, on 8 July in Belo Horizonte. In a result described by BBC Sport's [[Phil McNulty]] in 2019 as "a drama ... that will never be forgotten by anyone who witnessed it", Germany [[Brazil v Germany (2014 FIFA World Cup)|won the game 7–1]], inflicting Brazil's first competitive defeat at home for 39 years.<ref name="PhilMcNulty2019">{{Cite web |title=I was there – Brazil 1–7 Germany |last=McNulty |first=Phil |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=28 December 2019 |access-date=13 September 2021 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/50868769 |archive-date=13 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210913172025/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/50868769 |url-status=live }}</ref> They took the lead on 10 minutes, when Müller scored from a corner, unmarked by any Brazil players. They doubled their lead on 22 minutes through Klose, with his record-breaking 16th World Cup goal, before Kroos made it 3–0 on 25 minutes with a left-footed volley.<ref name="GuardianBrazilGermany">{{Cite web |title=Brazil 1–7 Germany: World Cup 2014 semi-final – as it happened |last=Glendenning |first=Barry |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=8 July 2014 |access-date=13 September 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jul/08/brazil-v-germany-world-cup-2014-semi-final-live-report |archive-date=26 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210526183614/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jul/08/brazil-v-germany-world-cup-2014-semi-final-live-report |url-status=live }}</ref> Kroos scored again 179 seconds later, from an [[Assist (association football)|assist]] by [[Sami Khedira]], before Khedira himself scored to make it 5–0 at half time. Schürrle scored twice in the second half to make it 7–0, before [[Oscar (footballer, born 1991)|Oscar]] scored Brazil's sole goal shortly before the end.<ref name="BBCBrazilGermanyReport">{{Cite web |title=Brazil 1–7 Germany |last=McNulty |first=Phil |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=8 July 2014 |access-date=13 September 2021 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/28102403 |archive-date=12 June 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210612022005/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/28102403 |url-status=live }}</ref> Simon Burnton of ''[[The Guardian]]'' later described Germany's play as being "of a savagery unwitnessed against significant opposition in the tournament's history".<ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup stunning moments: Germany humiliate Brazil 7–1 |last=Burnton |first=Simon |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=23 May 2018 |access-date=13 September 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2018/may/23/world-cup-stunning-moments-germany-humiliate-brazil-7-1 |archive-date=13 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210913172025/https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2018/may/23/world-cup-stunning-moments-germany-humiliate-brazil-7-1 |url-status=live }}</ref> Germany progressed to their eighth final, 12 years after their last and their second since [[German reunification]].{{efn|German reunification took place in October 1990, a few months after West Germany's victory in the 1990 World Cup.<ref>{{Cite web |title=German reunification: together into the great unknown – archive, October 1990 |last=Nelsson |first=Richard |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=5 October 2018 |access-date=26 September 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/from-the-archive-blog/2018/oct/05/german-reunification-october-1990 |archive-date=13 November 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201113160606/https://www.theguardian.com/world/from-the-archive-blog/2018/oct/05/german-reunification-october-1990 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}<ref name="Britanica"/>

