Argentina were drawn in [[2014 FIFA World Cup Group F|Group F]], alongside [[Bosnia and Herzegovina national football team|Bosnia and Herzegovina]], [[Iran national football team|Iran]] and [[Nigeria national football team|Nigeria]].<ref name="GroupAfterTwo">{{Cite web |title=World Cup 2014: How can Argentina, Nigeria, Iran and |last=Swinburne |first=Zander |work=[[The Independent]] |date=24 June 2014 |access-date=13 September 2021 |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/international/world-cup-2014-how-can-argentina-nigeria-iran-and-bosnia-herzegovina-qualify-group-f-9560459.html |archive-date=13 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210913080841/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/international/world-cup-2014-how-can-argentina-nigeria-iran-and-bosnia-herzegovina-qualify-group-f-9560459.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Their opening game took place on 15 June 2014 against Bosnia and Herzegovina, who were making their World Cup debut. It was the first game of the tournament at the Maracanã Stadium.<ref>{{Cite web |title=A Brief History of Brazil's Most Treasured World Cup Stadium |last=Byrnes |first=Mark |publisher=[[Bloomberg News]] |date=16 June 2014 |access-date=13 September 2021 |url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2014-06-16/a-brief-history-of-brazil-s-most-treasured-world-cup-stadium |archive-date=13 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210913080850/https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2014-06-16/a-brief-history-of-brazil-s-most-treasured-world-cup-stadium |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="BBCArgentinaBosnia">{{Cite web |title=World Cup: Argentina 2–1 Bosnia-Hercegovina |last=Sanghera |first=Mandeep |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=15 June 2014 |access-date=13 September 2021 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25285099 |archive-date=13 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210513193037/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25285099 |url-status=live }}</ref> Argentina took the lead on 3 minutes, when Bosnian defender [[Sead Kolašinac]] scored an [[own goal]] after a [[Free kick (association football)|free kick]] by [[Lionel Messi]] had been flicked on by [[Marcos Rojo]]. In the second half, Messi scored himself to double Argentina's lead with a powerful shot following a [[Push and run|one-two]] with [[Gonzalo Higuaín]]. [[Vedad Ibišević]] scored for Bosnia and Herzegovina with 6 minutes remaining, but Argentina held on for a 2–1 win.<ref name="BBCArgentinaBosnia"/> Their second game was against Iran at the [[Estádio Mineirão]] in [[Belo Horizonte]] on 21 June. [[Barney Ronay]] of ''The Guardian'' described Iran as producing a "stirring performance against an Argentina team of all the attacking talents", and the match remained goalless until the 90th minute.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Argentina 1–0 Iran |last=Ronay |first=Barney |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=21 June 2014 |access-date=13 September 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jun/21/argentina-iran-world-cup-2014-group-f-match-report |archive-date=10 January 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210110171348/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jun/21/argentina-iran-world-cup-2014-group-f-match-report |url-status=live }}</ref> Argentina took the win in injury time, when Messi scored a left-footed shot into the corner from outside the penalty area to seal qualification for the next round with a game to spare.<ref name="GroupAfterTwo"/><ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup: Lionel Messi magic denies Iran a share of the spoils |last=Gittings |first=Paul |publisher=[[CNN]] |date=21 June 2014 |access-date=13 September 2021 |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2014/06/21/sport/football/world-cup-argentina-iran-nigeria-bosnia-football/index.html |archive-date=2 March 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210302183628/http://edition.cnn.com/2014/06/21/sport/football/world-cup-argentina-iran-nigeria-bosnia-football/index.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Their final group game was on 25 June, against Nigeria in Porto Alegre. Messi gave Argentina the lead on 3 minutes, scoring on the rebound after [[Ángel Di María]]'s shot hit the goalpost. [[Ahmed Musa]] equalised for Nigeria a minute later with a right-footed curling shot, before Messi gave Argentina the lead again with a free kick from {{convert|25|yards|m}} out shortly before half time. Musa equalised once again 2 minutes into the second half, hitting the ball past goalkeeper [[Sergio Romero]] after a one-two with [[Emmanuel Emenike]], but Rojo scored with his knee 3 minutes later to seal a 3–2 win and first place in the final group table.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Nigeria 2–3 Argentina |last=Chowdhury |first=Saj |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=25 June 2014 |access-date=13 September 2021 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25285406 |archive-date=27 February 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210227094350/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/25285406 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Argentina's round-of-16 opponents were [[Switzerland national football team|Switzerland]], on 1 July at the [[Arena Corinthians]] in [[São Paulo]]. Argentina had the majority of the [[Ball possession|possession]] during the game, but BBC Sport's Jonathan Jurejko labelled their play "unconvincing", citing their failure to break down a Switzerland side who were playing defensively.<ref name="BBCArgentinaSwitzerland">{{Cite web |title=Argentina 1–0 Switzerland (aet) |last=Jurejko |first=Jonathan |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=1 July 2014 |access-date=14 September 2021 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/27990606 |archive-date=1 January 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150101043839/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/27990606 |url-status=live }}</ref> There were no goals during normal time, and it remained 0–0 until 2 minutes before the end of extra time when Messi set up Di María to score the winner past Swiss goalkeeper [[Diego Benaglio]]. Substitute [[Blerim Džemaili]] almost equalised for Switzerland immediately afterwards with a shot that hit the goalpost, but Argentina held on for a 1–0 win.<ref name="BBCArgentinaSwitzerland"/> Their quarter-final was against [[Belgium national football team|Belgium]] at the [[Estádio Nacional Mané Garrincha]] in [[Brasília]] on 5 July. Argentina scored the only goal of the game on 8 minutes through Higuaín, who scored from the edge of the penalty area after Di María's pass was deflected off Belgium's [[Jan Vertonghen]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Argentina 1–0 Belgium |last=Fifield |first=Dominic |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=5 July 2014 |access-date=14 September 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jul/05/argentina-belgium-world-cup-2014-quarter-final-match-report |archive-date=14 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210914203533/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jul/05/argentina-belgium-world-cup-2014-quarter-final-match-report |url-status=live }}</ref> Argentina played the [[Netherlands national football team|Netherlands]] in the semi-final on 9 July. There were no goals in the game, in either normal time or extra time, in what McNulty described as "120 tedious ... minutes that were in stark contrast to the spectacular shock" of the previous day's semi-final between Brazil and Germany.<ref name="BBCArgNetherlandsReport">{{Cite web |title=Netherlands 0–0 Argentina (2–4 on pens) |last=McNulty |first=Phil |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=9 July 2014 |access-date=14 September 2021 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/28122293 |archive-date=14 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210914204911/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/28122293 |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Ron Vlaar]] of the Netherlands took the first penalty, a low shot to the right, which was saved by Romero. The next three kicks, by Messi, [[Arjen Robben]] and [[Ezequiel Garay]], were all scored, before Romero made another save, diving to his right to keep out a high penalty from [[Wesley Sneijder]]. [[Sergio Agüero]], [[Dirk Kuyt]] and [[Maxi Rodríguez]] all scored their penalties, giving Argentina a 4–2 shoot-out win.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Holland v Argentina: World Cup 2014 semi-final – as it happened! |last=Murray |first=Scott |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=9 July 2014 |access-date=14 September 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jul/09/holland-argentina-world-cup-2014-semi-final-live |archive-date=14 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210914220902/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jul/09/holland-argentina-world-cup-2014-semi-final-live |url-status=live }}</ref> The press in Argentina called Romero's two saves the "hands of God", a reference to the [[The hand of God|"hand of God"]] goal scored by [[Diego Maradona]] in 1986.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Netherlands 0 Argentina 0 World Cup 2014: 'Sergio Romero's Hands of |work=[[The Independent]] |date=10 July 2014 |access-date=14 September 2021 |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/international/netherlands-0-argentina-0-world-cup-2014-sergio-romero-s-hands-god-how-world-reacted-argentina-reaching-world-cup-final-9596790.html |archive-date=14 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210914220901/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/international/netherlands-0-argentina-0-world-cup-2014-sergio-romero-s-hands-god-how-world-reacted-argentina-reaching-world-cup-final-9596790.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Argentina progressed to their fifth final, which was also their first since 1990.<ref name="Britanica"/>

==Match==
===Pre-match===
[[File:CFK y Angela Merkel 2.jpg|thumb|Chancellor of Germany [[Angela Merkel]] (left) attended the final. President of Argentina [[Cristina Fernández de Kirchner]] (right) did not attend due to her grandson's birthday and a case of pharyngo-laryngitis.|alt=Angela Merkel and Cristina Fernández de Kirchner pictured shaking hands]]
[[Nicola Rizzoli]], from Italy, was named as the referee of the final, together with fellow Italians Renato Faverani and Andrea Stefani as the assistant referees, and [[Carlos Vera]] and Christian Lescano from Ecuador as the [[Assistant referee (association football)#Fourth official|fourth]] and [[Assistant referee (association football)#Fifth official|fifth]] officials.<ref name="FinalReferee">{{cite web|url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2014/m=7/news=nicola-rizzoli-to-referee-final-2403526.html|title=Nicola Rizzoli to referee Final|website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association|date=11 July 2014|access-date=2 November 2017|archive-date=4 April 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180404073005/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2014/m=7/news=nicola-rizzoli-to-referee-final-2403526.html|url-status=dead}}</ref> Earlier in the 2014 World Cup, Rizzoli took charge of the Spain–Netherlands and Nigeria–Argentina matches in the group stage, and the Argentina–Belgium quarter-final. He had previously taken charge of the [[2010 UEFA Europa League final]] and the [[2013 UEFA Champions League final]]. He was also one of the referees at the [[2011 FIFA Club World Cup]], UEFA Euro 2012 and the [[2013 FIFA U-20 World Cup]]. He became the third Italian referee to take charge of a World Cup final, after [[Sergio Gonella]] in [[1978 FIFA World Cup final|1978]] and [[Pierluigi Collina]] in [[2002 FIFA World Cup final|2002]].<ref name="FinalReferee" />

A closing ceremony for the World Cup took place about an hour and forty minutes before the final.<ref name="FIFA.com">{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2014/m=7/news=closing-ceremony-to-celebrate-brazil-2014-in-style-2404018.html |title=Closing ceremony to celebrate Brazil 2014 in style |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |date=12 July 2014 |access-date=13 July 2014 |archive-date=12 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140712210012/http://www.fifa.com//worldcup/news/y=2014/m=7/news=closing-ceremony-to-celebrate-brazil-2014-in-style-2404018.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> A performance of two acts, the ceremony lasted about 20 minutes. The first act featured 22 [[samba]] dancers and a host of other performers, 32 of the dancers wearing dresses decorated in the colours of each of the participating teams. The second act featured musical performances headlined by Colombian singer [[Shakira]], and included singers [[Carlinhos Brown]], [[Wyclef Jean]], [[Alexandre Pires]] and [[Ivete Sangalo]] as well as guitarist [[Carlos Santana]].<ref name="NDTV">{{cite web |url=http://sports.ndtv.com/fifa-world-cup-2014/news/226851-la-la-la-shakira-to-set-world-cup-2014-final-ball-rolling |title=Shakira, Santana Electrify World Cup 2014 Closing Ceremony |publisher=[[NDTV]] |date=13 July 2014 |access-date=13 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140714055154/http://sports.ndtv.com/fifa-world-cup-2014/news/226851-la-la-la-shakira-to-set-world-cup-2014-final-ball-rolling |archive-date=14 July 2014 |url-status=dead |df=dmy-all }}</ref><ref name="CBC.ca">{{cite web |url=http://www.cbc.ca/sports/soccer/brazil2014/shakira-headlines-fairly-normal-world-cup-closing-ceremony-1.2705684 |title=Shakira headlines fairly normal World Cup closing ceremony |publisher=[[Canadian Broadcasting Corporation]] |date=13 July 2014 |access-date=13 July 2014 |archive-date=20 November 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161120053110/http://www.cbc.ca/sports/soccer/brazil2014/shakira-headlines-fairly-normal-world-cup-closing-ceremony-1.2705684 |url-status=live }}</ref> Brazilian supermodel [[Gisele Bündchen]] and [[Carles Puyol]], a member of Spain's winning team in 2010, unveiled the [[FIFA World Cup Trophy]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2014/m=7/news=puyol-gisele-to-unveil-trophy-prior-to-the-final-2402512.html |title=Puyol, Gisele to unveil Trophy before the Final |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |access-date=11 July 2014 |archive-date=9 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140709234924/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2014/m=7/news=puyol-gisele-to-unveil-trophy-prior-to-the-final-2402512.html |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Brazilian president [[Dilma Rousseff]] was present at the final, along with Russian president [[Vladimir Putin]], whose country hosted [[2018 FIFA World Cup|the next World Cup]],<ref name="Guardian" /> and [[Jacob Zuma]], the president of South Africa, the previous host country.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://citizen.co.za/209808/zuma-watch-world-cup-final/ |title=Zuma to watch World Cup final |agency=[[South African Press Association]] |work=[[The Citizen (South African newspaper)|The Citizen]] |date=13 July 2014 |access-date=13 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140715165954/http://citizen.co.za/209808/zuma-watch-world-cup-final/ |archive-date=15 July 2014|url-status=dead|df=dmy-all}}</ref> Representing the finalists, German president [[Joachim Gauck]] and chancellor [[Angela Merkel]] were in attendance,<ref>{{cite web |first=Marcelo |last=Camargo |url=http://agenciabrasil.ebc.com.br/en/geral/noticia/2014-07/germany-chancellor-and-president-watch-final-maracana |title=Germany chancellor and president to watch final at Maracanã |agency=[[Agência Brasil]] |publisher=[[Brazil Communication Company]] |date=25 January 2014 |access-date=11 July 2014 |archive-date=14 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140714155301/http://agenciabrasil.ebc.com.br/en/geral/noticia/2014-07/germany-chancellor-and-president-watch-final-maracana |url-status=live }}</ref> although Argentina's president [[Cristina Fernández de Kirchner]] could not attend because of both her grandson's birthday and a case of pharyngo-laryngitis.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/soccer-insider/wp/2014/07/13/argentinas-president-isnt-attending-the-world-cup-final-because-of-grandsons-birthday/ |title=Argentina's president isn't attending the World Cup final because of grandson's birthday |first=Marissa |last=Payne |date=13 July 2014 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |access-date=14 July 2014 |archive-date=14 July 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140714131930/http://www.washingtonpost.com/blogs/soccer-insider/wp/2014/07/13/argentinas-president-isnt-attending-the-world-cup-final-because-of-grandsons-birthday/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Other world leaders present included [[Viktor Orbán]] of Hungary, [[Ali Bongo Ondimba]] of Gabon and Antigua and Barbuda's [[Gaston Browne]].<ref name="Guardian">{{cite web|title=World Cup final guest list has bizarre and random look|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jul/13/world-cup-final-guest-list-2014|first=Owen|last=Gibson|work=[[The Guardian]]|date=13 July 2014|access-date=14 July 2014|archive-date=16 November 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181116095125/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jul/13/world-cup-final-guest-list-2014|url-status=live}}</ref> Several celebrities and athletes also attended the final, including [[Rihanna]], [[Daniel Craig]], [[Mick Jagger]], [[Ashton Kutcher]], [[David Beckham]], [[Tom Brady]], [[Olivier Dacourt]], [[LeBron James]], and [[Christian Vieri]].<ref name="Guardian"/> Former World Cup winners like [[Fabio Cannavaro]], [[Lothar Matthäus]], [[Daniel Passarella]], [[Marco Materazzi]] and [[Pelé]] were present as well.<ref name="Guardian"/><ref name="Washington Post">{{cite news|last1=Maese|first1=Rick|last2=Phillips|first2=Dom|title=2014 World Cup draws to a close in conflicted Brazil|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/sports/dcunited/2014-world-cup-draws-to-a-close-in-conflicted-brazil/2014/07/13/0b5fcc8e-0adb-11e4-929c-4cd4865c3725_story.html |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|access-date=13 July 2014| date=13 July 2014 |archive-date=13 December 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181213224232/https://www.washingtonpost.com/sports/dcunited/2014-world-cup-draws-to-a-close-in-conflicted-brazil/2014/07/13/0b5fcc8e-0adb-11e4-929c-4cd4865c3725_story.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup/10965689/World-Cup-2014-Best-selfies.html?frame=2974053 |title=World Cup 2014: Best selfies |work=[[The Daily Telegraph]] |access-date=17 July 2014 |archive-date=14 December 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181214141708/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup/10965689/World-Cup-2014-Best-selfies.html?frame=2974053 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.veooz.com/photos/KHKMgYC.html |title=Former internationals Lothar Matthaeus, Fabio Cannavaro and Daniel Passarella take a selfie the WC 2014 Final match |publisher=[[Veooz]] |access-date=17 July 2014 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20140726180225/http://www.veooz.com/photos/KHKMgYC.html |archive-date=26 July 2014 |url-status=dead |df=dmy-all }}</ref> Brazilian spectators at the game were largely supportive of the Germans as a result of the [[Argentina–Brazil football rivalry]], despite their team's heavy semi-final loss.<ref>{{cite magazine|last1=Young|first1=James|title=Brazil falls short, but its World Cup provides unforgettable theater|url=https://www.si.com/soccer/planet-futbol/2014/07/13/brazil-world-cup-final-germany-argentina-rio-maracana-memories|magazine=[[Sports Illustrated]]|date=13 July 2014|access-date=9 December 2017|archive-date=12 August 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170812141003/https://www.si.com/soccer/planet-futbol/2014/07/13/brazil-world-cup-final-germany-argentina-rio-maracana-memories|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last1=Brunner|first1=Cody|title=Argentina's World Cup final loss to Germany gives Brazil something to cheer about|url=https://sports.yahoo.com/news/argentina-s-world-cup-final-loss-to-germany-gives-brazil-something-to-cheer-about-015701180-soccer.html|publisher=[[Yahoo]]|date=13 July 2014|access-date=14 January 2017|archive-date=13 August 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140813101327/https://sports.yahoo.com/news/argentina-s-world-cup-final-loss-to-germany-gives-brazil-something-to-cheer-about-015701180-soccer.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Brazilians Go Back to Real Life|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2014/07/14/sports/worldcup/world-cup-2014-brazil-was-embarrassed-but-an-argentina-victory-would-have-been-intolerable.html?_r=0|work=[[The New York Times]]|date=13 July 2014|access-date=1 March 2017|archive-date=25 August 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180825004430/https://www.nytimes.com/2014/07/14/sports/worldcup/world-cup-2014-brazil-was-embarrassed-but-an-argentina-victory-would-have-been-intolerable.html?_r=0|url-status=live}}</ref>

Germany were forced to make a late change to their line-up when Khedira sustained a [[Calf (leg)|calf]] injury during the warm-up before the match. He was replaced in the line-up by [[Christoph Kramer]],<ref>{{Cite web |title=Germany replacement Kramer lasts half an hour |publisher=[[Eurosport]] |date=13 July 2014 |access-date=23 September 2021 |url=https://www.eurosport.com/football/world-cup/2014/germany-replacement-kramer-lasts-half-an-hour_sto4325018/story.shtml |archive-date=23 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210923190254/https://www.eurosport.com/geoblocking.shtml |url-status=live }}</ref> with Germany's team otherwise unchanged from that which started in their win over Brazil in the semi-final.<ref name="BBCBrazilGermanyReport"/><ref name="BBCReportOnTheFinal">{{Cite web |title=Germany 1–0 Argentina (aet) |last=McNulty |first=Phil |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=13 July 2014 |access-date=15 September 2021 |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/28181689 |archive-date=21 January 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160121051708/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/28181689 |url-status=live }}</ref> Argentina began the match with the identical team to that which started their semi-final against the Netherlands.<ref name="BBCArgNetherlandsReport"/><ref name="BBCReportOnTheFinal"/>

===First half===
[[File:Cámaras - Acción - 140713-8653-jikatu (14479326838).jpg|thumb|Lionel Messi running with the ball into the penalty area on nine minutes|alt=View from behind the goal of Messi with the ball, while two German defenders chase him and the goalkeeper stands guard|upright=1.2]]
Germany kicked off the match at 4&nbsp;pm local time (7&nbsp;pm [[UTC]]) in temperatures of {{convert|23|°C|°F|abbr=on}} with 65% humidity, in front of a crowd of 74,738 and an estimated global television audience of 1.013 billion.<ref name="GER-ARG line-ups"/><ref name="BBCReportOnTheFinal"/><ref name="2006coverage">{{cite web |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2015/m=12/news=2014-fifa-world-cuptm-reached-3-2-billion-viewers-one-billion-watched--2745519.html |title=2014 FIFA World Cup reached 3.2 billion viewers, one billion watched final |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |access-date=4 November 2017 |archive-date=22 December 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171222014250/http://www.fifa.com/worldcup/news/y=2015/m=12/news=2014-fifa-world-cuptm-reached-3-2-billion-viewers-one-billion-watched--2745519.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> The weather at [[Santos Dumont Airport]], {{convert|6.5|km|mi|sigfig=1}} from the stadium,{{efn|Distance measured using Google Maps distance calculator, between Rio de Janeiro Airport RJ Santos Dumont, coordinates {{Coord|22.913|S|43.167|W}} and Maracanã, coordinates {{Coord|22.912|S|43.232|W}}<!-- Coords rounded -->.<ref><!--[[Wikipedia:No original research|OR?]]-->{{Google maps |url=https://www.google.com/maps/place/Rio+de+Janeiro+Airport+RJ+Santos+Dumont/@-22.9129077,-43.1670911,18z |access-date=2 October 2021 |title=Rio de Janeiro Airport RJ Santos Dumont}}</ref><ref>{{Google maps |url=https://www.google.com/maps/place/Maracan%C3%A3/@-22.9121039,-43.2323445,17z |access-date=2 October 2021 |title=Maracanã}}</ref>}} was recorded as fair at the time of kick-off, becoming partly cloudy later in the evening.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Rio de Janeiro, Brazil Weather History – Jul 13, 2014 |publisher=[[The Weather Company]] |date= |access-date=2 October 2021 |url=https://www.wunderground.com/history/daily/br/rio-de-janeiro/SBRJ/date/2014-7-13 |archive-date=2 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211002092336/https://www.wunderground.com/history/daily/br/rio-de-janeiro/SBRJ/date/2014-7-13 |url-status=live }}</ref> Germany won a free kick 3 minutes into the game, when Rojo fouled Müller around {{convert|25|yards|m}} from the Argentina goal. The free kick was hit straight at the [[Glossary of association football terms#W|wall]], and Argentina launched a counterattack from the rebound. [[Pablo Zabaleta]] ran with the ball down the right before passing to Higuaín on the edge of the penalty area. He took a shot from a narrow angle, but the ball went wide of Neuer's left-hand goalpost. On 9 minutes, Messi outpaced Hummels on the Argentina right and ran into the penalty area. He attempted to find [[Ezequiel Lavezzi]] from the [[Glossary of association football terms#B|byline]] but Schweinsteiger reached it first and cleared.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute">{{Cite web |title=World Cup final 2014: Germany v Argentina – as it happened |last=Murray |first=Scott |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=13 July 2014 |access-date=19 September 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jul/13/world-cup-final-2014-germany-v-argentina-live-report |archive-date=10 June 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210610093929/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jul/13/world-cup-final-2014-germany-v-argentina-live-report |url-status=live }}</ref> Germany had considerably more of the early possession, but a German defensive error on 20 minutes gave Argentina what Jerry Hinnen of [[CBS Sports]] called "the best opportunity for either side" thus far. Kroos miscued a header, which left Higuaín [[Glossary of association football terms#O|one-on-one]] with Neuer, but the latter's shot from the edge of the penalty area went wide of the goal.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Look: Higuain misses golden opportunity to give Argentina a lead |last=Hinnen |first=Jerry |work=[[CBS Sports]] |date=13 July 2014 |access-date=19 September 2021 |url=https://www.cbssports.com/soccer/world-cup/news/look-higuain-misses-golden-opportunity-to-give-argentina-a-lead/ |archive-date=20 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210920212718/https://www.cbssports.com/soccer/world-cup/news/look-higuain-misses-golden-opportunity-to-give-argentina-a-lead/ |url-status=live }}</ref> On 23 minutes, Klose had a chance to score when the ball reached him following a cross by [[Philipp Lahm]], but Zabaleta dispossessed him before he could shoot.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/>

In the 30th minute, Lavezzi found space for Argentina on the right and crossed into the penalty area. Higuaín collected the ball and slotted it past Neuer into the goal.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/> He celebrated for several seconds, but the strike was disallowed as he had been in an [[Offside (association football)|offside]] position.<ref name="BBCReportOnTheFinal"/> Germany made a substitution shortly afterwards, taking off Kramer as a result of a head injury he had sustained 14 minutes previously.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Germany's Christoph Kramer asked referee: 'Is this the final?' |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=17 July 2014 |access-date=20 September 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jul/17/christoph-kramer-germany-concussion-world-cup-final-2014 |agency=[[Associated Press]] |archive-date=10 April 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170410133046/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jul/17/christoph-kramer-germany-concussion-world-cup-final-2014 |url-status=live }}</ref> He was replaced by Schürrle.<ref name="BBCReportOnTheFinal"/> Höwedes received a [[Penalty card#Yellow card|yellow card]] on 33 minutes for a tackle which connected with Zabaleta's shins, before Messi had a {{convert|20|yards|m|adj=on}} shot blocked by Schweinsteiger. A minute later, Germany launched an attack down the left through Müller, who passed to Schürrle. He hit a shot towards the top left of the goal, but Romero did a full-stretch dive to save it,<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/> despite having his eyeline blocked by Özil.<ref name="BBCReportOnTheFinal"/> Messi then had another opportunity, running with the ball into the Germany penalty area and kicking the ball towards goal. [[Jérôme Boateng]] prevented Argentina taking the lead with a goal-line clearance.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/> Shortly before half time, Höwedes received a Kroos corner in the penalty area, in what reporters for [[UEFA]] called the "best chance of the half", but his headed shot from an offside position struck the goalpost and it remained 0–0 at half time.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Götze goal wins Germany the World Cup |publisher=[[UEFA]] |date=13 July 2014 |access-date=20 September 2021 |url=https://www.uefa.com/european-qualifiers/news/0252-0d041909b876-94c8ce6b802f-1000--gotze-goal-wins-germany-the-world-cup/ |archive-date=20 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210920214806/https://www.uefa.com/european-qualifiers/news/0252-0d041909b876-94c8ce6b802f-1000--gotze-goal-wins-germany-the-world-cup/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Second half===
[[File:Germany and Argentina face off in the final of the World Cup 2014 -2014-07-13 (37).jpg|thumb|Miroslav Klose shooting at goal in the second half of the game|alt=View of Klose with the ball having just been kicked, with two Argentine defenders having contested the ball, while goalkeeper Romero awaits and another German player looks on]]
Argentina began the second half with Agüero on the field in place of Lavezzi. Higuaín had a shot in the first minute of the half after running down the right wing, but it went wide of Neuer's goal. Messi then had an opportunity to score when he was one-on-one with the goalkeeper a minute later, but his low shot on goal also went wide. Argentina had more possession than Germany in the opening 10 minutes of the half, and they had another chance on 50 minutes when Higuaín received a ball just inside the penalty area; he missed the ball with his attempted shot. In the 56th minute, Neuer took down Higuaín in the penalty area, but no penalty was given, despite Argentine commentators believing it should be given. No card was given either. Klose had Germany's first shot on goal of the second half on 59 minutes but his shot lacked power and was caught by Romero. Argentina had an opportunity to score through a [[Martín Demichelis]] header four minutes later, but Klose intercepted to send it behind. Shortly afterwards, [[Javier Mascherano]] brought down Klose after losing the ball to him, and was booked. His team-mate Agüero was also booked a minute later for a foul on Schweinsteiger. Midway through the second half, ''The Guardian''{{'s}} Scott Murray commented that the first half had been "goalless and brilliant", but the second was "goalless and ... well, not so much", although he noted that it remained an open game.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/>

Germany had a chance on 71 minutes, Müller, Schürrle and Özil all getting involved in a build-up which Murray described as "[[tiki-taka]]", but when Schürrle eventually reached the penalty area with only Romero to beat, he failed to control the ball properly and the goalkeeper claimed it. Messi had a shot on 75 minutes, following a run across the edge of the German penalty area, which he attempted to curl into the top left-hand corner of the goal; it missed the target. Germany appealed for a penalty with 10 minutes remaining, after a tussle close to the Argentina goalpost following a Lahm cross, but the referee awarded a [[goal kick]] to Argentina.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/> Kroos then had a chance for Germany following a [[Glossary of association football terms#L|lay-off pass]] by Özil, but he again missed the target.<ref name="BBCReportOnTheFinal"/> Both sides made substitutions in the final 5 minutes – [[Fernando Gago]] replacing [[Enzo Pérez]] for Argentina, and Götze came on for Klose for Germany.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/> This represented Klose's last appearance for Germany, and he remained the most prolific goalscorer for any country in World Cup history, until the [[2026 FIFA World Cup|2026 World Cup]], when [[Lionel Messi]] surpassed his record after scoring a brace [[2026 FIFA World Cup Group J#Argentina vs Austria|against Austria]].<ref>{{cite web | title=Messi becomes the World Cup's all-time leading scorer | work=FIFA | url=https://www.fifa.com/en/tournaments/mens/worldcup/canadamexicousa2026/articles/lionel-messi-all-time-top-scorer-argentina | access-date=30 June 2026 | archive-date=5 July 2026 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260705203524/https://www.fifa.com/en/tournaments/mens/worldcup/canadamexicousa2026/articles/lionel-messi-all-time-top-scorer-argentina | url-status=live }}</ref>

===Extra time===
[[File:Goldegotze.jpg|thumb|Mario Götze struck the winning goal in extra time|alt=Close-up view from the left of Götze hitting the ball, with the Argentine goalkeeper visible, partly obscured by defender Demichelis]]
Early in the first half of extra time, Schürrle received a pass in front of goal from Götze, but his close-range shot was stopped by Romero.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/> This was followed by a [[Glossary of association football terms#B|break]] upfield by Messi and Agüero with only two defenders covering, but Boateng was able to intercept and clear the danger. Five minutes later, a [[Chip (association football)|lob]] by [[Rodrigo Palacio]] over Neuer went just wide after the forward jumped on a mistake by Hummels in the German penalty area.<ref name="TelegraphMinuteByMinute"/> At the halfway point in extra time, Murray wrote that Argentina were "offering very little up front" and speculated that many of the players might be "thinking about penalties already".<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/> Alan Tyers of ''[[The Daily Telegraph]]'' noted that Messi did not "look quite the ticket" and that he had not been playing well since half time.<ref name="TelegraphMinuteByMinute">{{cite web |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup/10959517/World-Cup-final-2014-Germany-vs-Argentina-as-it-happened.html |title=World Cup final 2014, Germany vs Argentina: as it happened |date=13 July 2014 |work=[[The Daily Telegraph]] |access-date=14 July 2014 |archive-date=28 June 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180628183920/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup/10959517/World-Cup-final-2014-Germany-vs-Argentina-as-it-happened.html |url-status=live |first1=Alan |last1=Tyers |first2=Mehreen |last2=Khan}}</ref> In the second half of extra time, Germany broke the deadlock when Götze scored in the 113th minute. Schürrle raced past two defenders on the left before crossing into the penalty area, where Götze controlled the ball on his chest and then volleyed left-footed into the net.<ref>{{cite web |first=Daniel |last=Taylor |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jul/13/germany-argentina-world-cup-final-2014-match-report |title=Germany beat Argentina to win World Cup final with late Mario Götze goal |date=13 July 2014 |work=[[The Guardian]] |access-date=14 July 2014 |archive-date=9 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190509093653/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jul/13/germany-argentina-world-cup-final-2014-match-report |url-status=live }}</ref> Müller had a chance to double Germany's lead shortly afterward when he [[Dribbling|dribbled]] past two defenders, but his shot across the net was too wide. Late in extra time, Messi had an opportunity to equalise from a free kick within goal-scoring distance, but his attempt flew high over the crossbar.<ref>{{cite web |title= Messi's World Cup ends on a bitter note |date=13 July 2014 |url= http://www.cbsnews.com/news/messis-world-cup-ends-on-a-bitter-note/ |access-date= 22 July 2014 |agency= [[Associated Press]] |publisher= [[CBS]] |archive-date= 18 July 2014 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20140718153219/http://www.cbsnews.com/news/messis-world-cup-ends-on-a-bitter-note/ |url-status= live }}</ref> That proved to be the last action of the game as the final whistle was blown, confirming Germany's 1–0 victory.<ref name="GuardianMinuteByMinute"/><!--In addition to Klose, Germany captain [[Philipp Lahm]] and veteran defender [[Per Mertesacker]], a late substitute, announced that the final would also be their last match for [[Germany national football team|Germany]]. Combined, these three players had amassed 354 appearances for the national team between them.<ref>{{cite web |title= What happened to Germany's World Cup winners? |url= http://www.espn.com/soccer/blog/espn-fc-united/68/post/3073945/what-happened-to-germany-world-cup-winning-squad-of-2014-in-the-years-since-their-victory?src=com |access-date= 2 November 2018 |publisher= [[ESPN]] |archive-date= 6 November 2018 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20181106210734/http://www.espn.com/soccer/blog/espn-fc-united/68/post/3073945/what-happened-to-germany-world-cup-winning-squad-of-2014-in-the-years-since-their-victory?src=com |url-status= live }}</ref> He became the first substitute to score a World Cup-winning goal,<ref>{{cite episode |title=Match of the Day Live |series=2014 FIFA World Cup: World Cup Final |credits=Presenter: Gary Lineker |station=[[BBC One]] |airdate=13 July 2014 |time=4:07:29 |quote=Do you know, that's the first substitute to score a winning goal in a World Cup final? }}</ref> as well as the youngest player to score in a World Cup Final since German [[Wolfgang Weber]] in [[1966 FIFA World Cup final|1966]] (same age, 22).<ref name="Interesting stats 2014 World Cup Final">{{cite web |url=http://www.firstpost.com/sports/numbers-game-stats-germanys-fourth-world-cup-triumph-1617441.html |title=Numbers Game: All the stats from Germany's fourth World Cup triumph |newspaper=[[Firstpost]] |date=14 July 2014 |access-date=14 July 2014 |archive-date=2 October 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181002101405/https://www.firstpost.com/sports/numbers-game-stats-germanys-fourth-world-cup-triumph-1617441.html |url-status=live }}</ref>-->

