==Post-match==
[[File:Philipp Lahm lifts the 2014 FIFA World Cup.jpg|thumb|German captain Philipp Lahm lifts the World Cup trophy|alt=View of the celebrating German team on a podium behind a glass screen, Lahm holding the trophy aloft, while spectators with cameras stand behind]]
Germany's victory marked the first World Cup win by a European team in the [[Americas]], as well as the third successive European triumph after Italy and Spain's wins in 2006 and 2010.<ref name="Britanica"/><ref>{{Cite web |title=2014 World Cup Brazil |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |date= |access-date=24 September 2021 |url=https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2014brazil |archive-date=27 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210927021726/https://www.fifa.com/tournaments/mens/worldcup/2014brazil |url-status=live }}</ref> President Rousseff of Brazil presented the trophy to German captain [[Philipp Lahm]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www1.folha.uol.com.br/internacional/en/sports/worldcup/2014/07/1482711-dilma-rousseff-confirms-she-will-hand-the-trophy-to-the-world-cup-winner-and-says-boos-are-part-of-the-job.shtml |title=Dilma Rousseff Confirms She Will Hand the Trophy to the World Cup Winner and Says Boos "Are Part of the Job" |work=[[Folha de S.Paulo]] |access-date=11 July 2014 |archive-date=16 November 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181116103217/https://www1.folha.uol.com.br/internacional/en/sports/worldcup/2014/07/1482711-dilma-rousseff-confirms-she-will-hand-the-trophy-to-the-world-cup-winner-and-says-boos-are-part-of-the-job.shtml |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-soccer-brazil-rousseff-idUSKBN0FJ2GF20140714|publisher=[[Reuters]]|date=15 July 2014|access-date=8 August 2018|title=Brazil defeated pessimistic predictions for the World Cup – Rousseff|archive-date=8 August 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180808180112/https://www.reuters.com/article/us-soccer-brazil-rousseff-idUSKBN0FJ2GF20140714|url-status=live}}</ref> Alongside her during the trophy handover was [[List of presidents of FIFA|FIFA president]] [[Sepp Blatter]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.gettyimages.com/license/452120268|publisher=[[Getty Images]]|date=15 July 2014|access-date=8 August 2018|title=Philipp Lahm of Germany is presented with the World Cup trophy by Brazilian President Dilma Rousseff and FIFA President Joseph S. Blatter|archive-date=18 September 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210918095943/https://www.gettyimages.com/detail/news-photo/philipp-lahm-of-germany-is-presented-with-the-world-cup-news-photo/452120268|url-status=live}}</ref> As Lahm raised the trophy, the [[Conclusion (music)|outro]] of the tournament's official song "[[We Are One (Ole Ola)]]" was played. The goalscorer Götze was named as the official [[Player of the match|man of the match]] for the final, despite playing only the last two minutes of normal time and thirty minutes of extra time. Messi was awarded the [[FIFA World Cup awards#Golden Ball|Golden Ball]], FIFA's award for whom they considered the tournament's best player, and Neuer was given the [[FIFA World Cup awards#Golden Glove|Golden Glove]] for best goalkeeper.<ref>{{cite web |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140717062438/https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/awards/index.html |archive-date=17 July 2014 |title=Awards |website=FIFA.com |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |url=https://www.fifa.com/worldcup/awards/index.html |access-date=24 September 2021 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

[[Joachim Löw]], the [[Manager (association football)|manager]] of Germany, labelled his side's win as the culmination of a project which had begun ten years previously under his predecessor [[Jürgen Klinsmann]]. Speaking after the game, Löw said: "We'd not made this ultimate step before, but champions do what they will do. This team has developed a spirit which is unbelievable."<ref name="joachim-low-mario-gotze-germany-argentina-messi">{{Cite web |title=Joachim Löw: I told Mario Götze to 'show you are better than Messi' |last=Fifield |first=Dominic |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=14 July 2014 |access-date=24 September 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jul/14/joachim-low-mario-gotze-germany-argentina-messi |archive-date=8 May 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190508192119/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jul/14/joachim-low-mario-gotze-germany-argentina-messi |url-status=live }}</ref> His Argentine counterpart, [[Alejandro Sabella]], praised his players, describing them as "warriors", and felt that they had been unlucky to lose. He said that his team had had the better chances "but we didn't take them, and we only had to last another five minutes or so to reach penalties. The pain is immense."<ref name="joachim-low-mario-gotze-germany-argentina-messi"/> Götze expressed a sense of disbelief at his scoring of the winning goal, having not had the opportunity to play for much of the tournament. He said: "You score the goal and you don't really know what's happening. It hasn't been a simple tournament, or year, for me and I owe a lot to my family and my girlfriend who always believed in me. But a dream became reality here."<ref name="joachim-low-mario-gotze-germany-argentina-messi"/>

The German press praised the team for their overall performance in the tournament, contrasting it with the team's poor form a decade earlier. Christian Gödecke, writing in ''[[Der Spiegel]]'' wrote "Now Germany is world champion, and German football is barely recognisable. It's the perfect mix of virtue and magic, of hurrah and heave ho."<ref name="GermanPress">{{Cite web |title=World Cup final press reaction: Germany's win a 'fitting punchline' |last=Oltermann |first=Philip |author-link=Philip Oltermann |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=14 July 2014 |access-date=3 October 2021 |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jul/14/world-cup-final-press-reaction-germany-argentina-2014 |archive-date=3 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211003220235/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2014/jul/14/world-cup-final-press-reaction-germany-argentina-2014 |url-status=live }}</ref> Writers in the ''[[Süddeutsche Zeitung]]'' described the final as a "fitting punchline" to the tournament, while noting that "There won't be debates like there were in Rome in 1990, when the penalty that [[Andreas Brehme]] netted for the 1–0 victory was controversial and triggered conspiracy theories against FIFA amongst the Argentinians".<ref name="GermanPress"/> In Argentina, the press were downbeat but also indicated their pride in the team's achievement,<ref name="ElNacional">{{Cite web |title=Te Enteraste |last=Corporán |first=Leo |work=El Nacional |date=14 July 2014 |access-date=3 October 2021 |url=https://elnacional.com.do/te-enteraste-1755/ |language=es |archive-date=3 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211003220245/https://elnacional.com.do/te-enteraste-1755/ |url-status=live }}</ref> with many newspapers featuring a front-page picture of Messi after the match, alone or with his teammates.<ref>{{multiref2
|1={{cite news |page=1 |title=Argentina: Paleó hata el final |language=es |work=[[Clarín (Argentine newspaper)|Clarín]]}}
|2={{cite news |page=1 |title=Tristeza y Orgullo |language=es |work=[[Crónica (newspaper)|Crónica]]}}
|3={{cite news |page=1 |title=Orgullo Nacional |language=es |work=[[Diario Popular]]}}
|4={{cite news |page=1 |title=El amor es más fuerte |language=es |work=[[Página 12]]}}
|5={{cite news |page=1 |title=Fin de sueño Mundial |language=es |work=[[Los Andes (Argentine newspaper)|Los Andes]]}}
|6={{cite news |page=1 |title=Sin Copa, con orgullo |language=es |work=[[La Nación]]}}
|7={{cite news |page=1 |title=El esfuerzo no alcanzó |language=es |work=[[La Voz del Interior]]}}
|8={{cite news |page=1 |title=You made us proud |work=[[Buenos Aires Herald]]}}
|9={{cite news |page=1 |title=Gracias Mundialias |language=es |work=[[Olé (sports newspaper)|Olé]]}}}}</ref> Newspaper ''[[Clarín (Argentine newspaper)|Clarín]]'' lamented the team's missed chances and their denied penalty appeal.<ref name="ElNacional"/> Ezequiel Fernández Moores of ''[[La Nación]]'' thought Germany the deserved winners, and believed the referee correct in denying Higuaín's penalty appeal.<ref name="BrazilRevelsIrishTimes">{{Cite news |title=Brazil revels in Argentina's World Cup final misfortune |last=Hennigan |first=Tom |newspaper=[[The Irish Times]] |date=15 July 2014 |access-date=4 October 2021 |url=https://www.irishtimes.com/sport/soccer/international/brazil-revels-in-argentina-s-world-cup-final-misfortune-1.1865755 |quote=Higuaín |archive-date=8 December 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141208215304/http://www.irishtimes.com/sport/soccer/international/brazil-revels-in-argentina-s-world-cup-final-misfortune-1.1865755 |url-status=live }}</ref> The host country's rivalry with Argentina continued to feature in the Brazilian press after the game, sports daily ''[[Lance!]]'' using the headline "Tell Me How You Feel Now" in response to Argentine mocking of Brazil throughout the tournament. Speaking on his TV show, Maradona responded to this by contrasting Argentina's one-goal defeat to the 7–1 loss suffered by Brazil.<ref name="BrazilRevelsIrishTimes"/> A photograph by Chinese photographer Bao Tailiang of the ''[[Chengdu Economic Daily]]'', showing Messi staring at the World Cup Trophy after Argentina's defeat, won the Sports Singles award at the 2015 [[World Press Photo of the Year]] contest.<ref>{{cite web |date=12 February 2015 |title=World Cup final photo of Lionel Messi eyeing trophy wins prize |publisher=[[ESPN]] |access-date=3 October 2021 |url=https://www.espn.co.uk/football/league-name/story/2295953/headline |archive-date=3 October 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211003220239/https://www.espn.co.uk/football/league-name/story/2295953/headline |url-status=live }}</ref>

Germany's next major tournament was [[UEFA Euro 2016]], in which they reached the semi-final before [[UEFA Euro 2016 knockout phase#Germany vs France|losing 2–0]] to hosts France.<ref>{{Cite web |title=European Championship 2016 |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |date=15 October 2020 |access-date=8 October 2021 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/2016e.html |archive-date=12 June 2021 |website=[[RSSSF]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210612002331/http://www.rsssf.com/tables/2016e.html |url-status=live }}</ref> At the subsequent 2018 World Cup in Russia, Germany [[2018 FIFA World Cup Group F|failed to advance from the group phase]], finishing bottom of their group behind [[Sweden men's national football team|Sweden]], [[Mexico national football team|Mexico]] and [[South Korea national football team|South Korea]]. Germany secured only one win at the tournament, over Sweden, and lost to Mexico and South Korea, becoming the third successive World Cup holders to be eliminated in the group stage after [[Italy national football team|Italy]] in [[2010 FIFA World Cup|2010]] and [[Spain national football team|Spain]] in [[2014 FIFA World Cup|2014]], and also was eliminated in the early stages of a World Cup for the first time since [[1938 FIFA World Cup|1938]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Germany struck by the curse of the World Cup winner |first=Peter |last=Rutzler |work=[[i (British newspaper)|i]] |date=27 June 2018 |access-date=24 September 2021 |url=https://inews.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup/germany-be-struck-by-the-curse-of-the-world-cup-winner-166182 |archive-date=24 September 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210924165229/https://inews.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup/germany-be-struck-by-the-curse-of-the-world-cup-winner-166182 |url-status=live }}</ref> Germany would again fail to surpass the group stage in 2022 as well. Additionally, Germany has failed to keep a [[Shutout|clean sheet]] in every World Cup match since the 2014 final, up to and including their elimination from the [[2026 FIFA World Cup|2026 tournament]] in a penalty shoot-out against [[Paraguay national football team|Paraguay]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.foxsports.com/stories/soccer/world-cup-roundup-japan-brazil-netherlands-morocco-june-29 |title=World Cup Roundup: Paraguay Shocks Germany; Brazil Survives With Late Winner |publisher=Fox Sports |date=30 June 2026 |access-date=30 June 2026 |archive-date=30 June 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260630181909/https://www.foxsports.com/stories/soccer/world-cup-roundup-japan-brazil-netherlands-morocco-june-29 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Argentina's next major tournament was the [[2015 Copa América]], where they reached [[2015 Copa América Final|the final]] but were defeated by [[Chile national football team|Chile]] in a penalty shoot-out, followed by a rematch at the [[2016 Copa América]] at [[2016 Copa América Final|the final]] where they would also lose to them in a penalty shoot-out.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Copa América 2015 |last=Stokkermans |first=Karel |date=16 June 2016 |access-date=24 September 2021 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/2015sa.html |archive-date=8 January 2020 |website=[[RSSSF]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200108091305/http://www.rsssf.com/tables/2015sa.html |url-status=live }}</ref> In the 2018 World Cup, they advanced from their group in second place behind [[Croatia national football team|Croatia]], before being beaten in the round of 16 by eventual winners France.<ref>{{cite web|last=Stokkermans|first=Karel|title=World Cup 2018|url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/2018f.html|work=[[RSSSF]]|date=20 December 2022|access-date=28 April 2024|archive-date=30 June 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200630070249/http://rsssf.com/tables/2018f.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Argentina eventually won their third World Cup with a victory against France on penalties in the [[2022 FIFA World Cup final]].<ref name="Britanica"/>

